course: dictionary
lesson: 10526
title: Who is a typist?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A typist is a person who types, often as their job, such as typing letters and documents. The word ends in -ist, like a person who does something. A pianist plays the piano, and a typist types.
explain: Read the context: «The QWERTY order of keys comes from typewriters of the 1870s; a popular story says it slowed typists so the keys would not jam.» The words typewriters, keys and jam are clues. Typists were people using typewriters long ago, not computers.
explain: Anyone who types can be called a typist, such as «a fast typist». Try your meaning in the sentence: «She is a fast typist» means she types quickly. It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A typist is a person who types.
fact: «A fast typist» means someone who types quickly.
fact: Before computers, typists often worked on typewriters in big typing pools.
fact: A touch typist types without looking at the keys, and a two-finger typist uses just two fingers.
fact: In maths problems, typing speed is given in words per minute.
exercise: «She is a fast typist, and she finished the letters before lunch.» What is a typist? | A person who sells keyboards | A machine that prints paper | A room full of desks | *A person who types
exercise: «He is a two-finger typist.» What does two-finger suggest? | *He types using only two fingers | He has two jobs | He types with his toes | He types in two languages
exercise: A popular story says the QWERTY keys were arranged to slow typists so the keys would not jam. What does the word «popular» mean here? | Very expensive | *Known and repeated by many people | Hard to understand | Only used by one person
test: «Before computers, typists worked in big typing pools.» Where did typists often work? | *In big typing pools | In the school playground | At the seaside | On the moon
test: A typist types 60 words in one minute. How is this speed written? | 60 pages per day | *60 words per minute | 60 keys per second | 60 letters per hour
exam: Historians still argue about the real reason for the QWERTY order. What does this tell you? | The story is a complete lie | Historians know exactly why, and there is no argument | Typists chose the order themselves on purpose today | *Nobody is completely sure, so the popular story may not be the whole truth
